on-map-global-sections-degree-n}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:bbddbaa318612a70297c755078af3d60f8b5ab4e710b0c7ac57d84cfbbe62e48", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:16b4a29722a52663f190146c40f5036da8ede881b850482e332a7e5f44cab932", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nWe are going to use Lemma <REF_0001>\nto construct some canonical maps.\nSince <MATH_0001> we see that there\nare canonical maps\n<MATH_0002>\nThese maps are not isomorphisms in general, see the example in\nRemark <REF_0002>. The same example shows\nthat <MATH_0003> is <TEXTITOPEN_0001>not<TEXTITCLOSE_0001> an invertible sheaf on <MATH_0004> in\ngeneral. Tensoring with an arbitrary <MATH_0005>-module <MATH_0006>\nwe get maps\n<MATH_0007>\nThe maps (<REF_0003>)\non global sections give a map of graded rings\n<MATH_0008>\nAnd for an arbitrary <MATH_0009>-module <MATH_0010> the maps\n(<REF_0004>) give a graded module structure\n<MATH_0011>\nand via (<REF_0005>) also a <MATH_0012>-module structure.\nMore generally, given any graded <MATH_0013>-module\n<MATH_0014> we have <MATH_0015>. Hence we get maps\n<MATH_0016>\nOn global sections (<REF_0006>)\ndefines a map of graded <MATH_0017>-modules\n<MATH_0018>\nHere is an important fact which follows basically immediately from the\ndefinitions.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:a2061f5793a17fb2b39fbbc7be61cac796c95a614e08ab7768e3205816308538", "unit_id": "tag:01MM:prose-0002"}
